WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.376 --> 00:00:08.046
My wife grew up in Santa Rosa, California,

00:00:08.088 --> 00:00:10.423
and her best friend
was a woman named Joy Durand

00:00:10.465 --> 00:00:13.134
who lived on the northeast
outskirts of town.

00:00:14.094 --> 00:00:17.806
At midnight on the evening
of October 8, 2017,

00:00:17.847 --> 00:00:19.266
Joy's telephone rang.

00:00:20.183 --> 00:00:21.768
The voice said simply,

00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:24.354
"You must evacuate now.

00:00:24.354 --> 00:00:26.314
You don't have time to pack.

00:00:26.314 --> 00:00:27.691
Fire is coming."

00:00:28.858 --> 00:00:31.444
Joy gathered her parents
and her six-year-old son,

00:00:31.444 --> 00:00:33.029
and they hurried outside.

00:00:33.029 --> 00:00:37.158
The wildfire, which at that point
was just over the ridge from Joy's house,

00:00:37.158 --> 00:00:40.161
sounded like a jet engine
going at full throttle.

00:00:41.538 --> 00:00:44.416
The family got in their car, drove away,

00:00:44.457 --> 00:00:48.003
and within minutes,
their home had burned to the ground,

00:00:48.044 --> 00:00:51.423
incinerated by the blowtorch flames
of the Tubbs Fire.

00:00:52.757 --> 00:00:57.596
All night long, my wife got texts
and messages from friends and family,

00:00:57.637 --> 00:00:59.764
and while we didn’t know
the whole situation,

00:00:59.806 --> 00:01:03.893
it was clear that a disaster
was unfolding in her hometown.

00:01:04.519 --> 00:01:09.024
No one thought that fire could penetrate
so deeply into the city.

00:01:09.649 --> 00:01:11.026
But on that evening,

00:01:11.026 --> 00:01:13.403
22 people lost their lives

00:01:13.403 --> 00:01:17.699
and over five thousand homes
and structures were destroyed.

00:01:19.492 --> 00:01:24.414
Wildfire is a large and growing challenge
to the West and to the world.

00:01:25.248 --> 00:01:28.418
What happened in Santa Rosa
is becoming all too common.

00:01:28.460 --> 00:01:34.382
Paradise in 2018, Australia in 2020,
Europe in 2022.

00:01:34.424 --> 00:01:35.675
And fire experts say

00:01:35.717 --> 00:01:37.844
that we should get ready
for more Santa Rosas

00:01:37.886 --> 00:01:41.097
because it's likely to get worse
before it gets better.

00:01:43.266 --> 00:01:47.187
Now beyond the major costs

00:01:47.228 --> 00:01:50.649
in lives and property and the economy,

00:01:50.649 --> 00:01:54.194
there's also a big
carbon impact to wildfire.

00:01:54.194 --> 00:01:59.407
In fact, the additional carbon
from California's 2020 wildfires

00:01:59.407 --> 00:02:01.326
was greater than the carbon

00:02:01.326 --> 00:02:04.162
that Californians had worked
so hard to save

00:02:04.162 --> 00:02:05.997
over the previous two decades.

00:02:06.706 --> 00:02:09.209
The largest fires are called megafires.

00:02:09.250 --> 00:02:11.753
These are the ones
that burn over 100,000 acres

00:02:11.795 --> 00:02:16.341
with the intensity that can threaten
aquifers and biodiversity,

00:02:16.383 --> 00:02:19.219
and even cause forest conversion,

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:22.639
in which trees are so damaged
that they just don't grow back.

00:02:22.681 --> 00:02:24.808
These fires are getting worse as well.

00:02:24.849 --> 00:02:29.187
In fact, eight of the ten
largest megafires in California history

00:02:29.187 --> 00:02:31.398
have happened
over just the last five years.

00:02:32.774 --> 00:02:35.610
Many people feel overwhelmed
by this situation.

00:02:35.610 --> 00:02:36.903
I know I did.

00:02:37.821 --> 00:02:42.450
So two years ago,
working with great people,

00:02:43.618 --> 00:02:47.622
I closed a chapter
of my life in aerospace

00:02:47.664 --> 00:02:49.290
and I started a new journey

00:02:49.332 --> 00:02:54.379
to see if I could understand
the wildfire crisis better

00:02:54.421 --> 00:02:56.506
and what could be done about it.

00:02:56.548 --> 00:03:01.177
I started working with leaders
from firefighting and philanthropy,

00:03:01.219 --> 00:03:04.681
entrepreneurship,
science, tribal communities,

00:03:04.723 --> 00:03:08.893
and together we cofounded an organization
called Megafire Action,

00:03:08.935 --> 00:03:13.732
whose sole purpose
is to solve the megafire crisis.

00:03:13.732 --> 00:03:17.485
And I've come to believe
that if we take a holistic approach,

00:03:17.485 --> 00:03:22.282
we have an opportunity
to establish a new relationship with fire,

00:03:22.282 --> 00:03:24.200
to work safely with fire

00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:27.203
and potentially to solve
this wicked problem.

00:03:28.288 --> 00:03:30.957
The path forward has three solutions.

00:03:31.875 --> 00:03:35.628
The first is fire-adapted communities,

00:03:35.670 --> 00:03:38.423
the second is resilient landscapes

00:03:38.465 --> 00:03:41.718
and the third is
innovative fire management.

00:03:41.760 --> 00:03:45.096
So for the first,
fire-adapted communities,

00:03:45.138 --> 00:03:51.519
what we need to do is to clear the brush
and the vegetation from homes,

00:03:51.561 --> 00:03:54.189
from the immediate proximity of homes.

00:03:54.189 --> 00:03:58.818
Next, we need to
use fire-resistant materials

00:03:58.818 --> 00:04:01.529
in the homes and the roofs.

00:04:01.529 --> 00:04:05.033
And then the third thing
is to, where possible,

00:04:05.033 --> 00:04:08.578
protect against embers, flying embers,

00:04:08.578 --> 00:04:14.542
by protecting the home from openings
like your air vents and your chimneys.

00:04:15.418 --> 00:04:18.671
Now communities have
a strong shared interest

00:04:18.713 --> 00:04:23.510
to perform this work along
the exterior boundary of the community

00:04:23.551 --> 00:04:25.386
because it reduces the chance

00:04:25.428 --> 00:04:28.973
that fire will penetrate
deeply into the city,

00:04:29.015 --> 00:04:31.142
as it did in Santa Rosa.

00:04:31.184 --> 00:04:34.604
The second solution
is resilient landscapes.

00:04:34.604 --> 00:04:37.857
And if you take one lesson
from this talk, it's this.

00:04:37.857 --> 00:04:39.818
In order to solve the megafire crisis,

00:04:39.818 --> 00:04:44.197
we need to bring our western landscapes
back into a healthy balance

00:04:44.197 --> 00:04:50.537
by reducing the overgrown brush and trees
in the wildlands and the forest.

00:04:50.537 --> 00:04:53.289
Here we're finally starting
to take to heart the wisdom

00:04:53.331 --> 00:04:55.708
of the Indigenous peoples of the Americas

00:04:55.750 --> 00:04:58.586
who knew that fire was
a natural part of the landscape

00:04:58.628 --> 00:05:03.424
and who introduced low-intensity fire,
good fire, on a regular basis,

00:05:03.466 --> 00:05:04.801
at the right times.

00:05:05.385 --> 00:05:07.262
When this is done well,

00:05:07.303 --> 00:05:10.932
as around the communities
of South Lake Tahoe,

00:05:10.974 --> 00:05:13.852
then it can actually divert a megafire

00:05:13.893 --> 00:05:18.648
as bad as the one
that they experienced in 2021.

00:05:18.648 --> 00:05:22.944
It can also potentially prevent
a megafire from occurring,

00:05:22.944 --> 00:05:26.906
which is what some people think
happened in Napa of last year.

00:05:27.699 --> 00:05:30.952
But the scale of this challenge is huge.

00:05:30.952 --> 00:05:35.790
There are hundreds of millions of acres
of wildland in the American forests.

00:05:35.832 --> 00:05:37.876
The Forest Service
hopes to reduce the risk

00:05:37.917 --> 00:05:42.422
on 50 million high-priority acres
over the next ten years.

00:05:42.463 --> 00:05:47.176
But the challenge is that they're only
accomplishing a few million acres a year.

00:05:47.218 --> 00:05:49.345
And so we're not at the pace or scale

00:05:49.387 --> 00:05:52.515
that we need to do
to address this challenge.

00:05:52.557 --> 00:05:54.142
So what do we need to do?

00:05:54.183 --> 00:05:56.102
Well, the first thing that we need to do

00:05:56.144 --> 00:06:02.275
is to essentially hire,
train and retain many more workers

00:06:02.275 --> 00:06:06.696
who will be doing the hard work
of defusing the time bombs

00:06:06.696 --> 00:06:08.448
inside our western forests.

00:06:08.448 --> 00:06:10.742
We need to pay them well.

00:06:10.742 --> 00:06:13.328
We need to support them
with full-time work

00:06:13.328 --> 00:06:16.831
and we need to protect them
from liability where necessary.

00:06:16.873 --> 00:06:20.585
The next thing we need to do
is to empower them with innovation.

00:06:20.627 --> 00:06:25.632
And right now there are
an incredible number of great companies

00:06:25.673 --> 00:06:30.386
and organizations that are working
to bring this innovation to reality.

00:06:30.428 --> 00:06:32.639
Just one of these companies
is called BurnBot,

00:06:32.680 --> 00:06:34.307
and what they're hoping to do

00:06:34.349 --> 00:06:38.686
is to make controlled burns
safer and faster.

00:06:38.728 --> 00:06:41.981
They're going to establish
a burned perimeter around an area

00:06:41.981 --> 00:06:44.359
with the remotely piloted rover

00:06:44.359 --> 00:06:46.986
and then they can bring a drone
into the middle of it,

00:06:46.986 --> 00:06:51.574
drop some fire into it
and burn that interior area safely.

00:06:51.574 --> 00:06:53.660
It's a brilliant but a simple idea.

00:06:53.660 --> 00:06:56.955
And I think we're going to need
a lot more of this kind of innovation

00:06:56.955 --> 00:06:59.791
if we're going to scale up
to the to the size of the problem

00:06:59.832 --> 00:07:01.084
that we have before us.

00:07:01.960 --> 00:07:04.671
Now the first two solutions
that I've talked to you about

00:07:04.712 --> 00:07:06.130
are pretty well agreed.

00:07:06.172 --> 00:07:10.843
The third one, innovative fire management,
is somewhat more controversial,

00:07:10.885 --> 00:07:17.684
but I think it offers huge potential
to address and solve the wildfire problem.

00:07:18.017 --> 00:07:22.689
What we need is innovation and technology
that can rapidly detect and assess fire

00:07:22.689 --> 00:07:25.566
and then quickly put it out
when it gets bad.

00:07:25.566 --> 00:07:27.902
And here, speed is paramount,

00:07:27.902 --> 00:07:31.614
because when you're
in the worst kind of fire days --

00:07:31.614 --> 00:07:35.410
these are the hottest,
driest and windiest fire days --

00:07:35.410 --> 00:07:39.872
if you can't bring fire management
resources to a fire very quickly,

00:07:39.914 --> 00:07:43.751
then it is likely that you
won't be able to contain that fire.

00:07:43.793 --> 00:07:45.503
Here we need to look at the example

00:07:45.545 --> 00:07:49.007
of the Quick Reaction Force
of Southern California,

00:07:49.048 --> 00:07:52.635
which is really designed
in some ways for these toughest days.

00:07:52.677 --> 00:07:55.763
The QRF is a public-private partnership,

00:07:55.805 --> 00:07:58.558
which now has three Chinook helicopters.

00:07:58.599 --> 00:08:02.020
These are the big ones
with the two rotors on the top.

00:08:02.061 --> 00:08:06.733
And each of them can drop
up to 3000 gallons of water on a fire,

00:08:06.733 --> 00:08:08.026
day or night,

00:08:08.026 --> 00:08:09.986
and they can do it very precisely.

00:08:09.986 --> 00:08:12.947
They can also refill
up to six times an hour

00:08:12.947 --> 00:08:15.199
so they can bring
a lot of mass to the problem.

00:08:16.492 --> 00:08:18.786
Over the course of two years
of demonstrations,

00:08:18.786 --> 00:08:22.498
the QRF has demonstrated
that this model has great potential,

00:08:22.540 --> 00:08:26.878
that, in fact, if you can bring
a lot of fire management resource

00:08:26.919 --> 00:08:28.504
to a fire very quickly,

00:08:28.546 --> 00:08:32.884
you can get on top of fires
before they get big and unmanageable.

00:08:32.925 --> 00:08:36.179
And this opens up
an exciting potential future.

00:08:36.220 --> 00:08:42.268
Imagine this model
with a series of larger drone vehicles

00:08:42.310 --> 00:08:47.023
distributed across the landscape
in higher-fire-risk areas.

00:08:47.023 --> 00:08:50.693
Imagine these vehicles
positioned periodically

00:08:50.693 --> 00:08:52.361
along utility lines

00:08:52.361 --> 00:08:56.491
or in areas of higher fire-risk
deep in the forest.

00:08:57.116 --> 00:09:01.245
Such a distributed network,
if connected to the right sensor system,

00:09:01.245 --> 00:09:02.872
could offer a future

00:09:02.914 --> 00:09:06.250
in which we are able
to really put out fires

00:09:06.292 --> 00:09:09.504
on the worst fire days,
and even in remote communities.

00:09:10.922 --> 00:09:16.886
Companies like Rain and Joby Aviation
are working on this vision today.

00:09:17.720 --> 00:09:20.515
The sensor system
will also be very important

00:09:20.556 --> 00:09:22.809
for the resolution of this problem.

00:09:22.850 --> 00:09:24.602
The system -- which will entail

00:09:24.644 --> 00:09:28.773
both ground-based,
aerial and space-based platforms --

00:09:28.773 --> 00:09:32.068
won't be important just because
we're going to be able to see

00:09:32.068 --> 00:09:35.530
and detect fire where and when it pops up,

00:09:35.530 --> 00:09:37.365
but also because it'll enable us

00:09:37.365 --> 00:09:40.701
to differentiate
between good and bad fire,

00:09:40.701 --> 00:09:43.412
low-intensity fire
and high-intensity fire,

00:09:43.412 --> 00:09:48.543
which is very important because we need
more lower-intensity fire on the landscape

00:09:48.584 --> 00:09:50.795
to rejuvenate our forests,

00:09:50.837 --> 00:09:52.380
just as much as we need to know

00:09:52.421 --> 00:09:55.383
where and when fire risk
is becoming higher intensity.

00:09:56.092 --> 00:10:00.555
Fire is a prism through which
we can see the future

00:10:00.596 --> 00:10:03.766
of humanity's relationship
with natural systems.

00:10:03.808 --> 00:10:06.769
Working together
and supported by technology,

00:10:06.811 --> 00:10:10.690
we can build a world in which communities
are resilient to wildfire

00:10:10.690 --> 00:10:14.610
and in which forests
are brought back into a healthy balance.

00:10:14.610 --> 00:10:17.113
Ultimately, we can build a future,

00:10:17.113 --> 00:10:20.575
in which we don't just manage fire
to protect human life and property,

00:10:20.575 --> 00:10:24.203
but also to protect the biosphere
for global carbon emissions

00:10:24.203 --> 00:10:26.289
and for biodiversity.

00:10:26.330 --> 00:10:30.376
Ultimately, we need to become
the crew of Spaceship Earth.

00:10:30.418 --> 00:10:31.752
So let's get to work.

00:10:31.794 --> 00:10:33.337
Let's learn from fire

00:10:33.379 --> 00:10:35.756
and let's build a resilient,
sustainable future.

00:10:36.257 --> 00:10:37.717
Thank you.

00:10:37.758 --> 00:10:39.802
(Applause)

